        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.234,
            0.087,
            0.89,
            0.191
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "You might have observed many types of crystals in everyday life, such as rock salt crystals (Fig. 5.7a) or sugar crystals (Fig. 5.7b) that grow while making candy sugar (called mishri in Hindi). Snowflakes are also crystals, formed when water vapour freezes in the air. Even frost on windows is formed when water vapour turns into ice crystals (Fig. 5.7c). This is how crystals form naturally."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.062,
            0.18,
            0.213,
            0.263
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 3,
              "bbox": [
                0.062,
                0.18,
                0.213,
                0.263
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_6_image_3.jpg",
              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_caption",
              "index": 4,
              "bbox": [
                0.125,
                0.266,
                0.148,
                0.28
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "(b)"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.234,
            0.193,
            0.89,
            0.311
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In laboratories, the process of forming crystals from a saturated solution is called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "crystallization",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". This method can be used for the separation of two solids, in which one of the compounds is present in small quantity and both are soluble in the same solvent. This method can also be used for purification of solids. The principle of purification by crystallization is based on the differences in the solubility of a substance at different temperatures."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.061,
            0.28,
            0.212,
            0.366
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 6,
              "bbox": [
                0.061,
                0.28,
                0.212,
                0.366
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_6_image_6.jpg",
              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_caption",
              "index": 7,
              "bbox": [
                0.125,
                0.367,
                0.148,
                0.38
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "(c)"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.266,
            0.315,
            0.549,
            0.333
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Try creating your own crystals."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.236,
            0.34,
            0.519,
            0.359
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Activity 5.3: Let us prepare"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.246,
            0.361,
            0.732,
            0.413
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1. Collect a sample of copper sulfate (blue vitriol). If copper sulfate is unavailable, you can use common salt as an alternative."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.755,
            0.323,
            0.812,
            0.339
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Note"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.748,
            0.343,
            0.875,
            0.401
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Sulfuric acid is required for the crystallization of only some salts."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.064,
            0.387,
            0.211,
            0.444
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Fig. 5.7: Crystals of (a) rock salt, (b) candy sugar, and (c) frost"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.235,
            0.415,
            0.889,
            0.448
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Safety first",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ": Copper sulfate is toxic. Perform the experiment under adult supervision and do not touch it with your bare hands."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.035,
            0.447,
            0.27,
            0.696
          ],
          "content": [
