                0.304,
                0.883,
                0.512,
                0.899
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "(d) Crystals of copper sulfate"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 23,
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.902,
            0.486,
            0.919
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Fig. 5.8: Steps involved in the process of crystallization"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 24,
          "bbox": [
            0.551,
            0.451,
            0.89,
            0.587
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2. Take 1 g of copper sulfate and place it in a 100 mL beaker. Add 25 mL of water to the beaker and add a drop of dilute sulfuric acid. Gently heat the mixture in a water bath while stirring constantly. Sulfuric acid helps in making pure crystals by preventing unwanted reactions."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 25,
          "bbox": [
            0.543,
            0.588,
            0.889,
            0.638
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Safety first",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ": It is advised that the teacher should add a drop of sulfuric acid. Handle sulfuric acid very carefully!"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 26,
          "bbox": [
            0.555,
            0.638,
            0.889,
            0.689
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3. Gradually, add more copper sulfate until the solution becomes saturated (Fig. 5.8a)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 27,
          "bbox": [
            0.556,
            0.689,
            0.889,
            0.757
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "4. Filter the hot solution to remove insoluble impurities (Fig. 5.8b). Collect it in a clean beaker and cover it with a watch glass."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 28,
          "bbox": [
            0.556,
            0.758,
            0.89,
            0.877
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "5. Allow the solution to cool slowly without disturbing it (Fig. 5.8c). It gives enough time to the particles in the solution to come together, resulting in the formation of larger, shiny, well-shaped and blue-coloured crystals."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 29,
          "bbox": [
            0.556,
            0.877,
            0.89,
            0.929
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "6. Filter the crystals, rinse them with cold water and allow them to dry on a watch glass (Fig. 5.8d)."
            }
          ]
        },
